{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11126 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Gluconate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a pathway involving hypercalcemia. The evidence suggests that Calcium Gluconate is linked to Hypercalcemia Disease, which in turn is associated with other calcium salts such as Calcium Carbonate and Calcium Acetate. These calcium salts are known to contribute to the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys, a hallmark of Nephrocalcinosis. The presence of hypercalcemia, often resulting from excessive calcium intake or impaired renal excretion, creates a milieu conducive to calcium crystallization and deposition in renal tissues. The integrated evidence highlights a mechanism where calcium salts, when present in excess, may promote the development of Nephrocalcinosis, particularly in individuals with hypercalcemia. This relationship underscores the role of calcium metabolism in renal pathology, with Calcium Gluconate acting as a mediator in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis.",
